{"subject":"Physiology","sourceTitle":"All In One Online","title":"PrepLadder · 11.Previous Year Questions","deckName":"created by tele @Neet_pg_bot::prep::Qbank::Physiology::11.Previous_Year_Questions","questions":[{"id":"all-in-one-1760436537090","topic":"PrepLadder · 11.Previous Year Questions","number":1,"stem":"Which of the following is an incorrect match?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gastrin - G cells from stomach"},{"label":"B","text":"Motilin - Mo cells from duodenum and jejunum"},{"label":"C","text":"CCK - D cells of duodenum and pancreas"},{"label":"D","text":"Secretin - S cells of duodenum and jenjunum"}],"answer":"C","answerText":"CCK - D cells of duodenum and pancreas","solutionHtml":"olid black; vertical-align:top\"&amp;gt;\n\t\t\t<p><strong>Hormone</strong></p>\n\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Secreting Cells</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Location</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Function </strong></p>\n</td>\n\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>CCK (Cholecystokinin)</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>I cells</p>\n<p>(Option C)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Duodenum</p>\n<p>Jejunum</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Stimulates</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pancreatic <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"0f160e4e42169277718771b715516a\">enzyme</span> secretion</li>\n<li>Pancreatic <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"2311c3b4ac16927771831d4c919043\">bicarbonate</span> secretion</li>\n<li>Gallbladder <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"ca2289e348169277718516017eb2ba\">contraction</span> (Release of bile)</li>\n<li>Growth of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"938890efd616927771870f4a611546\">exocrine</span> pancreas</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Inhibits</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gastric emptying</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Gastrin</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>G cells</p>\n<p>(Option A ruled out)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Stomach (antrum) Duodenum</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Stimulates:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gastric acid secretion</li>\n<li>Mucosal growth</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Secretin</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>S cells</p>\n<p>(Option D ruled out)</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Duodenum</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Stimulates:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pepsin secretion</li>\n<li>Pancreatic <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"2311c3b4ac16927771831d4c919043\">bicarbonate</span> secretion</li>\n<li>Biliary <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"2311c3b4ac16927771831d4c919043\">bicarbonate</span> secretion</li>\n<li>Growth of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"938890efd616927771870f4a611546\">exocrine</span> pancreas</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Inhibits:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gastrin release and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"12347b15191692777188bc86d226e1\">gastric</span> acid secretion</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Motilin</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Mo cells (Option B ruled out)</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Duodenum and jejunum</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Stimulates:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gastric motility</li>\n<li>Intestinal motility</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n\n\n<p> </p><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Guyton and Hall Textbook of Physiology, 14th edition, Pg 792</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436537092","topic":"PrepLadder · 11.Previous Year Questions","number":2,"stem":"The correct sequence of events during vomiting are? 1. Relaxation of lower esophageal sphincter 2. Strong contractions in the stomach 3. Inspiration against a closed glottis 4. Relaxation of the pyloric sphincter 5. Relaxation of upper esophageal sphincter 6. Reverse peristalsis in the small intestine","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6,4,2,5,1,3"},{"label":"B","text":"6,4,3,2,1,5"},{"label":"C","text":"4,6,2,1,3,5"},{"label":"D","text":"4,6,2,5,3,1"}],"answer":"B","answerText":"6,4,3,2,1,5","solutionHtml":"<ol>\n<li><strong>Reverse <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"69d8ba3cb3169277719543eceaf072\">Peristalsis</span> in the Small Intestine</strong>:\n\n\t<ul>\n<li>Reverse <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"69d8ba3cb3169277719543eceaf072\">peristalsis</span> pushes intestinal contents back into the stomach, possibly introducing <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"cf978047b61692777183c0569f21f2\">bile</span> into the vomit.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Relaxation of Pyloric Sphincter</strong>:\n\t<ul>\n<li>In severe cases, the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"0319b53dbd16927771968db216b352\">pyloric sphincter</span> relaxes, facilitating the expulsion of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"12347b15191692777188bc86d226e1\">gastric</span> and duodenal contents.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Inspiration Against a Closed Glottis</strong>:\n\t<ul>\n<li>A deep breath is taken while the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"427c7571fc1692777188fbb8492d21\">glottis</span> remains closed, creating <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"befa0a72121692777193663f2ca836\">negative pressure</span> in the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"3c6021096816927772007954fcc158\">thoracic</span> cavity.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Strong Contractions in the Stomach</strong>:\n\t<ul>\n<li>Abdominal muscles and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5b42867b61169277718692205c2447\">diaphragm</span> contract, increasing <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"65137b05df1692777190f7da6e176d\">intragastric</span> pressure, propelling contents upward.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Relaxation of Lower Esophageal <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"1a73a42f071692777199a59120f494\">Sphincter</span> (LES)</strong>:\n\t<ul>\n<li>LES relaxes, allowing stomach contents to move into the esophagus.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Relaxation of Upper Esophageal <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"1a73a42f071692777199a59120f494\">Sphincter</span> (UES)</strong>:\n\t<ul>\n<li>UES relaxes, allowing <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"58a09a3d07169277720271320338d4\">vomitus</span> to exit through the mouth</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p> </p>\n<table cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Step</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Description</strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>1. Relaxation of LES</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>LES relaxes to allow stomach contents to move into the esophagus.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>2. Strong Contractions in Stomach</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Abdominal and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5b42867b61169277718692205c2447\">diaphragm</span> muscles contract to propel stomach contents upward.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>3. Inspiration Against Closed Glottis</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Deep breath with <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"427c7571fc1692777188fbb8492d21\">glottis</span> closed creates <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"befa0a72121692777193663f2ca836\">negative pressure</span> for expulsion.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>4. Relaxation of UES</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>UES relaxes, allowing <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"58a09a3d07169277720271320338d4\">vomitus</span> to exit the mouth.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>5. Reverse <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"69d8ba3cb3169277719543eceaf072\">Peristalsis</span> in Small Intestine</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Reverse <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"69d8ba3cb3169277719543eceaf072\">peristalsis</span> pushes intestinal contents back into the stomach.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>6. Relaxation of Pyloric Sphincter</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid #000000; border-left:1px solid #000000; border-right:1px solid #000000; border-top:1px solid #000000; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>In severe vomiting, <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"0319b53dbd16927771968db216b352\">pyloric sphincter</span> relaxes, aiding in expulsion.</p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Ganong&rsquo;s Review of Medical Physiology, 26th Edition, Pg 490</div>","tags":""},{"id":"all-in-one-1760436537094","topic":"PrepLadder · 11.Previous Year Questions","number":3,"stem":"Following complete ileal and partial jejunal resection, the patient is most likely to develop?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Folic acid deficiency"},{"label":"B","text":"Vitamin B12 deficiency"},{"label":"C","text":"Iron deficiency"},{"label":"D","text":"Reduced absorption of carbohydrates"}],"answer":"B","answerText":"Vitamin B12 deficiency","solutionHtml":"36caf06\"&amp;gt;ileum of the small <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"7840cca3ed16927771901d2f6cb32b\">intestine</span> and requires an <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a6795c876e169277719057a1099465\">intrinsic</span> factor for its absorption.\n\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Absorption of Vitamins</strong>:</p>\n<table cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse; width:736px\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Type </strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Vitamin</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Primary <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5fcb9396d1169277718069dd7fa788\">Absorption</span> Site</strong></p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:1px solid black; vertical-align:top\">\n<p><strong>Mechanism of <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5fcb9396d1169277718069dd7fa788\">Absorption</span> </strong></p>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Fat-Soluble</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>A</p>\n<p>D</p>\n<p>E</p>\n<p>K</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Small <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"7840cca3ed16927771901d2f6cb32b\">intestine</span> (Duodenum, mainly jejunum).</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Require <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"cf978047b61692777183c0569f21f2\">bile</span> salts for emulsification.</li>\n<li>Incorporated into mixed micelles.</li>\n<li>Absorbed by <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"c72d29ec4116927771942153c0d252\">passive</span> <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"9c760dcb641692777186f4cda5dc95\">diffusion</span> into enterocytes.</li>\n<li>Packaged into chylomicrons for transport.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Water-Soluble</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>B Vitamins</p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Jejunum and Ileum.</p>\n<p>(B12 specifically in ileum)</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>Various mechanisms include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Active transport</li>\n<li>Passive diffusion</li>\n<li>Carrier-mediated transport</li>\n<li>Sodium-dependent transport</li>\n<li>B12 requires an <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a6795c876e169277719057a1099465\">intrinsic</span> factor</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<p>C</p>\n</td>\n<td style=\"border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; vertical-align:top\">\n<ul>\n<li>Sodium-dependent <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"74c9c0942a1692777180b936bbbfcc\">active transport</span> at low concentrations.</li>\n<li>Passive <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"9c760dcb641692777186f4cda5dc95\">diffusion</span> at high concentrations.</li>\n</ul>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Absorption of Iron:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Most dietary iron is <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5a046503cb169277720259dec3f2be\">ferric</span> (Fe³⁺), and the absorbed <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"a1b06f401f1692777203a53393ba98\">form</span> is <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"ffb2e30f4b1692777202053635c365\">ferrous</span> (Fe²⁺).</li>\n<li>Fe³⁺ is <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"10c83adb331692777197f775bec1aa\">reduced</span> to Fe²⁺ by <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"12347b15191692777188bc86d226e1\">gastric</span> secretions and <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"6afb2667fe16927771975a8db1378b\">reductase</span> activity in enterocytes.</li>\n<li>Iron <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5fcb9396d1169277718069dd7fa788\">absorption</span> occurs mainly in the <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"280e59e9331692777186f3beac2c41\">duodenum</span> via <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"9c9c29820b169277718670a8b16a3b\">divalent</span> metal <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"850b296dd316927772005b651f6e87\">transporter</span> 1 (DMT1</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Absorption of carbohydrates:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Maximum <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5fcb9396d1169277718069dd7fa788\">absorption</span> occurs in <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"4b145c8d1b1692777190177246177d\">jejunum</span> &amp;gt; <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"280e59e9331692777186f3beac2c41\">duodenum</span> = ileum. No <span class=\"customMeta\" data-dictid=\"5fcb9396d1169277718069dd7fa788\">absorption</span> in the colon</li>\n</ul><br/><br/><div class=\"quiz-references\">Ganong&rsquo;s Review of Medical Physiology, 26th edition, Page 445, 470, 478</div>","tags":""}]}